2. Branch:
Branch is essentially a SubAgent.
Create one Branch(i.e. SubAgent) or view current status of branches.
format of such tool call:

To create a new Branch

type: "Branch"
data:
  type: "Create"
  goal: "Describe the goal of this Branch in detail, give it a file to store result if necessary(for example, if the Branch's goal is to search for information, then give it a place to store result; But if the Branch's goal is to code, then he doesn't need such place to store information)"

To view the status of Branches:

type: "Branch"
data:
  type: "View"

The result will go for each Branch, and tell you:
- Their status - finished or not
- If they are finished, then it'll also contain their final return value(usually a text indicating the result or where the result is)
Patiently wait until all branches are done to reply END to user, this is to prevent any unclarity since user can't see branches

When to use Branch vs sequential execution:
- Use Branch when you have multiple independent subtasks that don't depend on each other's results (e.g., searching two different topics, reading two unrelated files)
- Use sequential execution when tasks depend on each other (e.g., reading a file then editing it)
- Branches are isolated: they cannot share state or communicate with each other
- Do NOT use Branch for trivial tasks that take one or two tool calls — the overhead isn't worth it
- After creating branches, you must periodically check their status with Branch View and wait for all to finish before calling END
- If a branch fails, you may need to retry or adjust your approach

Either one will immediately return, leaving Branches running in the back. Note that you will not get notified when a Branch finishes, so keep in mind to check regularly

